  recall: [
    { q: "Define preseptal and orbital cellulitis by their relation to the orbital septum.", a: "Preseptal: infection of the lid/periorbital tissue anterior to the orbital septum; globe not affected (aka periorbital cellulitis). Orbital: infection of orbital soft tissue posterior to the septum, involving fat and muscles; vision- and life-threatening.", cite: "Lecture 6 · p. 4–5, 36" },
    { q: "Which signs separate orbital from preseptal cellulitis? (exam item)", a: "Fever, pupil defect (APD), limited/painful eye movement (ophthalmoplegia), proptosis, plus swollen lid; also decreased VA, colour vision, fields, chemosis, optic disc edema. Preseptal has none of the pupil, motility or proptosis signs.", cite: "Guidelines · p. 2 · Lecture 6 · p. 20, 46, 59 · Audio · 16:42" },
    { q: "Name the three main sources of preseptal cellulitis with two examples each.", a: "Direct inoculation (laceration, strabismus surgery, insect bite); spread from nearby local infection (URI, sinusitis, dacryocystitis, acute hordeolum); hematogenous spread (otitis media, pneumonia).", cite: "Lecture 6 · p. 6–9" },
    { q: "List the risk factors for preseptal cellulitis.", a: "Hordeola, chalazia, bug bites, trauma-related lesions, lesions from recent surgery near the lids, lesions from oral procedures, dacryocystitis.", cite: "Lecture 6 · p. 11" },
    { q: "How do you treat preseptal cellulitis in an adult outpatient, and what if allergic to penicillin?", a: "Amoxicillin/clavulanate (Augmentin) 500/125 mg PO TID × 10 d (mild–moderate) or 875/125 mg BID × 10–14 d (severe), with follow-up; warm compresses; polymyxin B/bacitracin if conjunctivitis; decongestants if sinusitis. Penicillin allergy/MRSA: Bactrim DS or doxycycline 100 mg BID (not in children/pregnancy/nursing). Clindamycin and ceftriaxone are alternatives.", cite: "Lecture 6 · p. 27–31, 34" },
    { q: "When is preseptal cellulitis hospitalised, and which IV drugs are used?", a: "Moderate–severe disease, toxic patient, noncompliant patient, child under 5, no improvement or worsening after a few days. IV ampicillin + sulbactam (Unasyn), ceftriaxone, vancomycin if MRSA suspected.", cite: "Lecture 6 · p. 32–33" },
    { q: "Give the causes of orbital cellulitis and the single most common one.", a: "Bacterial sinusitis complication (most cases, mostly ethmoid sinusitis); extension from periorbital structures, sinuses and teeth (ask about dental work); direct infection after trauma or surgery (lid, strabismus, retinal); hematogenous spread of bacteremia.", cite: "Lecture 6 · p. 39–40" },
    { q: "Describe the work-up of orbital cellulitis.", a: "Case history (trauma/surgery, ENT/systemic infection, tooth pain/abscess, stiff neck or mental change, diabetes/immunosuppression); full eye exam (APD, EOM limitation/pain, proptosis, IOP, colour vision, optic nerve/fundus) plus vitals, mental status, neck; labs (CBC >15,000, blood culture, sinus/abscess culture; no needle aspiration of the orbit; LP if meningeal signs); imaging essential: high-resolution CT with contrast, axial and coronal; MRI for cavernous sinus.", cite: "Lecture 6 · p. 47–50" },
    { q: "How is orbital cellulitis treated? (exam item)", a: "Hospitalise all patients; IV broad-spectrum antibiotics STAT (ampicillin-sulbactam a good start) for 1–2 weeks then oral 2–3 weeks; anaerobes: penicillin G, cefoxitin, metronidazole, clindamycin, chloramphenicol; MRSA: vancomycin, cefotaxime, TMP-SMX, clindamycin; fungal: amphotericin B; surgical drainage if no response in 2–3 days, VA drops, APD, progressing proptosis, abscess not shrinking; craniotomy for brain abscess; consult pediatrics/ID/ENT.", cite: "Lecture 6 · p. 52–57 · Guidelines · p. 2" },
    { q: "List the five CT stages of orbital involvement.", a: "I preseptal cellulitis; II inflammatory orbital edema; III subperiosteal abscess; IV orbital abscess; V cavernous sinus thrombosis.", cite: "Lecture 6 · p. 51" },
    { q: "What can untreated orbital cellulitis lead to, and how often?", a: "Vision loss in 11% of cases; sinus thrombosis (1%) and meningitis (2%), which can be life-threatening.", cite: "Lecture 6 · p. 40" },
    { q: "Define poliosis and give its genetic and acquired causes.", a: "Localized patch of white hair from decreased/absent melanin in the bulb or follicle. Genetic: piebaldism, Waardenburg, tuberous sclerosis. Acquired: inflammatory, neoplastic (melanocytic, may be the first sign of melanoma), medications, vitiligo, VKH, alopecia, sarcoidosis, blepharitis.", cite: "Lecture 6 · p. 61–65" },
    { q: "Describe vitiligo and its management.", a: "Autoimmune acquired depigmentation, usually bilateral on the lids, before age 20, progressive, asymptomatic; thyroid abnormalities most common; family history 30%; 1–2% of people; white well-demarcated macules/patches enlarging outward. Plan: family history, rule out syndromes, camouflage, sun protection, amino-acid colour blending, topical steroids, dermatology consult, PUVA, narrow-band UVB, grafting, depigmentation.", cite: "Lecture 6 · p. 69–74" }
  ]
